<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Winter War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1939">1939</a>–<a href="/years/1940">1940</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Winter War was fought between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> from November 1939 to March 1940, after Finland refused Soviet demands for territory. Finnish forces inflicted heavy losses on the much larger <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow-peace-treaty">Moscow Peace Treaty</a> of March 1940 forced Finland to cede <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karelia">Karelia</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyborg">Viipuri</a>, and other areas, while keeping its independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/winter-war.jpg" alt="Winter War" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_Finnish_Maxim_M-32_machine_gun_nest_during_the_Winter_War.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Until the early 19th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> was the eastern part of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. Russia conquered it in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finnish-war">Finnish War</a> of 1808 to 1809 and made it the autonomous Grand Duchy of Finland. Late in the 19th century Russia tried to assimilate the duchy, attempts that ruined relations and strengthened support for self-determination in Finland. In the late 1930s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a> sought to regain provinces lost after 1917 and wanted more security for Leningrad, only 32 km from the Finnish border.</p><p>In April 1938 an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkvd">NKVD</a> agent, Boris Yartsev, suggested that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> cede or lease islands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-finland">Gulf of Finland</a>, which Finland refused. On 26 November 1939 the Soviet side reported the shelling of a border post near the village of Mainila. Finnish and Russian historians have concluded that it was a false flag operation carried out from the Soviet side.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 30 November 1939 Soviet forces invaded with 21 divisions, about 450,000 men, and bombed Helsinki. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> expelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> on 14 December 1939. Field Marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-gustaf-emil-mannerheim">Carl Gustaf Emil Mannerheim</a> became commander-in-chief. Soviet leaders had expected victory within weeks, but the land was forest, lake and swamp with few roads. Finnish ski troops in white snow capes struck Soviet columns, notably at Suomussalmi and the Raate Road, and Finnish soldiers improvised the Molotov cocktail against tanks. The winter was exceptionally cold, with a record low of −43 °C on 16 January 1940.</p><p>On the Karelian Isthmus the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finns">Finns</a> held the Mannerheim Line, a string of strong-points that was weaker than Soviet propaganda claimed. Stalin was displeased with the results of December and in late January 1940 the Soviets ended the puppet Terijoki Government and offered to negotiate with the Finnish government. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> reorganised, it renewed its offensive in February and broke the defences. By 5 March it had entered the suburbs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyborg">Viipuri</a>. Franco-British plans to intervene did not mature, since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> gave no right of passage, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> accepted the Soviet terms.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Under the Moscow Peace Treaty Finland ceded the Karelian Isthmus and most of Ladoga Karelia, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyborg">Viipuri</a>, about nine per cent of its territory and 13 per cent of its economic assets. The Soviet gains exceeded its pre-war demands, yet Finland kept its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. Historians differ over whether the war was a Soviet victory or a failure to absorb Finland. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>'s poor showing led to reforms in the spring of 1940 and encouraged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> to believe that an invasion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> would succeed. After the 15-month Interim Peace, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-barbarossa">Operation Barbarossa</a> began in June 1941 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/continuation-war">Continuation War</a> between Finland and the Soviet Union followed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Winter War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When Finland refused Soviet demands for territory, the Soviet Union attacked on 30 November 1939, starting the <b>Winter War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-finland">Republic of Finland · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact of August 1939 the Soviet Union invaded eastern Poland, fought the costly <b>Winter War</b> against Finland and annexed the Baltic states and Bessarabia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The false flag operation gave it a casus belli for launching the <b>Winter War</b> four days later.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-26">26 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Soviet forces attacked Finland, starting the <b>Winter War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-30">30 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Winter War</b>, the Soviet Union in March 1940 combined the Karelian Autonomous Soviet Socialist Republic with most of the territory ceded by Finland, creating a full union republic with Petrozavodsk as capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/karelo-finnish-soviet-socialist-republic">Karelo-Finnish Soviet Socialist Republic</a></li></ul></section>
